# Naming Conventions

## Files

Use:

```text
customer.service.ts
customer.repository.ts
customer.controller.ts
customer.schema.ts
auth.middleware.ts
```

## Variables

Use camelCase:

```ts
const customerName = "";
const orderStatus = "";
```

## Functions

Use camelCase:

```ts
createCustomer()
updateOrder()
```

## Classes

Use PascalCase:

```ts
CustomerService
OrderRepository
```

## Interfaces

Prefix with I:

```ts
interface ICustomer
interface IOrder
```

## Types

Use descriptive names:

```ts
type CustomerResponse
type OrderDTO
```

---

# Validation Rules

Use Zod for all external inputs.

Never trust client-side data.

Example:

```ts
const createCustomerSchema = z.object({
 name: z.string().min(3),
 email: z.email()
});
```

Validate:

* Request body.
* Query params.
* Route params.
* Environment variables.

Never perform manual validation when Zod can handle it.

---

# Error Handling

Use centralized error handling.

Create a custom AppError class.

Example:

```ts
export class AppError extends Error {
 constructor(
  message: string,
  public statusCode = 400
 ) {
  super(message);
 }
}
```

Always:

* Throw business exceptions.
* Handle unexpected errors.
* Return standardized responses.

Never:

* Leak internal errors.
* Return Prisma errors directly.

---

# Global Error Middleware

Implement a centralized error middleware.

Example:

```ts
app.use(errorMiddleware);
```

Avoid repetitive try/catch blocks when middleware can handle the error.

---

# Security Best Practices

Always:

* Hash passwords using bcrypt.
* Use environment variables.
* Configure CORS correctly.
* Validate all inputs.
* Sanitize all inputs.
* Implement rate limiting.
* Use Helmet.
* Use secure HTTP headers.

Recommended packages:

```bash
helmet
cors
bcrypt
express-rate-limit
```

Never:

* Store plain text passwords.
* Expose secrets.
* Log sensitive information.
* Trust client data.

---

# Authentication & Authorization

Use JWT Authentication.

Implement:

* Access Tokens.
* Refresh Tokens.
* Role-Based Access Control (RBAC).

Example Roles:

```ts
ADMIN
EMPLOYEE
CUSTOMER
```

Protect all private routes using middleware.

---

# Logging

Use structured logging.

Recommended:

```bash
pino
```

Log:

* Errors.
* Requests.
* Critical operations.

Never log:

* Passwords.
* Tokens.
* Sensitive customer data.

